I’ll inspect the specimen metadata, pattern grid, and the frozen PXRD tool guidance first, then build and refine a complete multiphase GSAS-II model. I’ll keep the search chemistry-led and stop once all coherent peak families are assigned.

The chemistry points first to Mg–W–O phases, but I’m treating that only as a prior. I’m now extracting the observed peak families and querying the frozen COD index for exact Mg/W/O references (including MgO and WO₃), before committing any phase inventory.

The observed families now support a complete ordinary inventory: wolframite-type MgWO₄ is the major phase, while the WO₃ triplet families and rocksalt MgO reflections are both present as residual precursors. I’m setting up controlled GSAS-II branches to select the WO₃ polymorph by whole-pattern agreement, then I’ll refine the accepted three-phase model rather than continue searching.

The whole-pattern branches distinguish the room-temperature monoclinic WO₃ model: COD 1528915 is already materially better (Rwp 17.51% before profile refinement) than triclinic 2106293 (18.83%) and orthorhombic 2107312 (20.28%); the P2₁/m model is much worse. I’m checking the remaining exact-composition WO₃ references, including an alternate monoclinic setting, before freezing that choice.

The alternate WO₃ model (COD 2311041) is the defensible choice: it gives the best checkpoint fit and comes from a neutron structure with normal W–O geometry. The superficially close 1528915 model contains 1.575 Å O–O contacts, so I rejected it despite its similar peak positions. Coordinate trials on the accepted topology are being rolled back when they require shifts larger than the stated reference-mismatch scale; I’m now refining broadening one supported term at a time.

The remaining residual is dominated by relative-intensity errors, not unindexed peaks. A physically supported correction is isotropic displacement: the frozen model registration normalizes every site to a shared phase Uiso, and the specimen settings then assign one phase value. I’m replacing unconstrained site-by-site U trials with common, bounded phase values (0.003–0.020 Å²), which is both replayable and consistent with the experiment model.

The accepted model is now fully physical and all residual maxima coincide with its three reflection families; there is no coherent fourth family. Common phase Uiso values and the instrument/profile terms are within the laboratory bounds. I’m doing one final pass from the smooth 30-term background checkpoint, then I’ll freeze the project and export all deliverables from that exact state.

The physical full-grid checkpoint is frozen at Rwp 8.320% (Rp 5.904%). I’m now running the same consistency checks the evaluator uses: CIF parsing/geometry, exact fit-grid reproduction, and a fresh GSAS-II replay from `final.gpx` rather than its cached calculated array.

The three-phase model is complete: MgWO₄, monoclinic WO₃, and MgO. I’m doing the final replay consistency check now, then I’ll write the uncertainty/evidence report and verify every deliverable.

The saved project reproduces the submitted profile exactly on replay: 8,301 points, Rwp 8.31998%, and a maximum calculated-profile difference of only \(5\times10^{-11}\) counts. I’m recording the model-selection evidence and the limits on fraction precision in the report.

Completed the three-phase refinement:

- MgWO₄: **77.02 wt%**
- Monoclinic WO₃: **15.05 wt%**
- MgO: **7.93 wt%**

Full-pattern fit: **Rwp 8.3200%**, Rp 5.9045%, GOF 2.9122. The project replay reproduced all 8,301 calculated points and phase fractions.

